  "title": "Erdős Problem #282",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $A\\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ be an infinite set and consider the following greedy algorithm for a rational $x\\in (0,1)$: choose the minimal $n\\in A$ such that $n\\geq 1/x$ and repeat with $x$ replaced by $x-\\frac{1}{n}$. If this terminates after finitely many steps then this produces a representation of $x$ as the sum of distinct unit fractions with denominators from $A$.\nDoes this process always terminate if $x$ has odd denominator and $A$ is the set of odd numbers? More generally, for which pairs $x$ and $A$ does this process terminate?",
    "sha256": "383084b2e0951a32895cf8843f84141d035b43b89d66ad238346887015cd658d",
